sections.s2_technical:
**Target contexts where type is inferred:**

Variable declaration: `Supplier<String> s = () -> "RELIANCE";` — inferred as Supplier.

Method parameter: `execute(() -> "RELIANCE")` — if `execute` takes `Supplier<String>`, lambda becomes Supplier.

Return statement: `return () -> "RELIANCE";` — if method return type is `Callable<String>`, lambda becomes Callable.

Cast: `(Supplier<String>) () -> "RELIANCE"` — explicitly forced to Supplier.

**The ambiguity problem:**

```java
// Two overloads accepting different functional interfaces with SAME lambda signature:
void process(Supplier<String> s) { }
void process(Callable<String> c) { }

// process(() -> "RELIANCE");  // COMPILE ERROR! Ambiguous!
// Both Supplier<String> and Callable<String> accept () -> String
// Compiler cannot decide which overload to call

// FIX: explicit cast
process((Supplier<String>) () -> "RELIANCE");  // calls Supplier overload
process((Callable<String>) () -> "RELIANCE");  // calls Callable overload
```

**Why Supplier and Callable are different but have compatible lambda signatures:** `Supplier.get()` returns T with no checked exception. `Callable.call()` returns T and declares `throws Exception`. A lambda `() -> "x"` matches BOTH because it does not throw.

